Scale-to-fit (CWindow::fitTransform): a window tagged `fit`, and every dock card,
whose client does not follow its configured size (Waydroid's fixed Android display)
is drawn scaled into its box, aspect kept and centred, instead of cropped. Covers the
surface tree's texture boxes and UVs, pointer / touch / tablet input (mapped back
through the same scale; the letterbox margin is a miss) and commit damage (repaints
the whole window). Inactive while the client's size matches, so well-behaved
windows render 1:1 exactly as before.

- A fitted window keeps its box below the client's minimum size: the commit-time
  clamp, which grows a floating window about its centre, is skipped for it, since the
  oversized surface is drawn scaled instead (it pushed a corner-parked PiP of Firefox
  off the monitor).
- A fitted/dock window never gets Hyprland's flat blur fill for a client smaller than
  its box (it was unwarped and showed around a letterboxed Waydroid card).
- A PiP (tag `pip`) is told it is decoration:keystone_pip_zoom (default 3) times its
  box (realToReportSize), so the client lays out for a big virtual screen of the
  box's own shape and scale-to-fit shrinks that into the box; the box can be resized.
- A back dock card (tag `dockscale<P>`) is told the FRONT card's size, box / P%, and
  drawn shrunk by scale-to-fit, so moving back in the pile never re-lays it out.

